Degree Plan: Phase 1
In this assignment/lab, we'll build a simple app for planning an undergraduate degree.

We'll build on this app for at least one more assignment, so we'll focus on making it easy to modify and update.

Requirements
For this phase of the assignment, your app must meet the following requirements:

A user must be able to enter, update, and edit the list of courses they plan to take.

Courses have:

A department string, such as CS

A course number, such as 6018

There must be a list of degree requirements.

Each degree requirement must visually indicate whether it is satisfied by the courses in the user's list.

If all requirements are satisfied, the app should indicate that in some way.

For now, your app must support these types of degree requirements:

Specific course: The student must take a particular course.

Example: You must take CS 101.

One course from a set: The student must take at least one course from a given set.

Example: You must take PHIL 101 or SOC 101.

Aside: Functions and Functional Programming are great
As a challenge, I recommend embracing a functional programming style as much as possible.

For example, consider replacing code like this:

var ret = listOf<Whatever>()
for (x in something) {
    ret.add(x.method())
}
with the functional equivalent:

val ret = something.map { it.method() }
Chat with your friends, TA, or professor about why the second version might be preferable.

Finishing Up — Read Before Starting
Since we'll be building more features on top of this app, we want to make sure the code is in a good place after this phase.

Split up large Composables into smaller pieces.

Use state hoisting so that you can define @Preview @Composable previews and see your Composables in action without running the entire app.

I recommend writing a @Preview at the same time you write the @Composable itself!

Write unit tests for any business logic, such as checking whether degree requirements are satisfied.

Try to replace loops with functional alternatives when possible.